<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanandaj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sinneh, Senneh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanandaj is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> province. Founded in the 1630s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soleyman-khan-ardalan">Soleyman Khan Ardalan</a> as the seat of the Ardalan principality, it was long known as Sinneh. It remains a centre of Kurdish culture and is known for its carpets and woodwork.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanandaj.jpg" alt="Sanandaj" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Γεωργός · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanandaj_from_Abidar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Sinna first appears in records from the 14th century CE. Before that the main city of the region was Sisar, also called Sisar of Sadkhaniya, or Sisar of the hundred springs, whose exact location is unknown; it has been proposed that Sinna is a contracted form of Sadkhaniya.</p><p>The name Sisar disappears in the 14th century, and Sinna replaces it in the works of Hamdallah Mustawfi, who refers to a mountain and a pass of that name. The Kurdish historian Sharaf al-Din Bitlisi mentions that in 1580 an Ardalan ruler named Timur Khan held a land grant that included Sinna and the earlier Ardalan capital of Hasanabad.</p><p>The local historian Ali-Akbar Munshi Waqayi-Nigar wrote in 1892/3 that Sinna was founded by the Ardalan ruler Soleyman Khan on the site of an earlier settlement, giving a chronogram that corresponds to 1046 AH, or 1636-7 CE. The town grew greatly under Aman Allah the Great, who reigned from 1797 to 1825. In the 19th century it was a lively commercial centre, exporting oak galls, tragacanth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and carpets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sanandaj is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and of its county and district. It lies between the Qishlaq river, a tributary of the Diyala, and Mount Awidar, which separates it from Hasanabad. Its people are mainly Kurdish, most follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, and Central Kurdish is the vernacular of daily life while Persian is the language of education and government. It is regarded as a historic centre of Kurdish cultural and intellectual life, with traditions of music, poetry and carpet weaving. Carpet making is the biggest industry.</p><p>In the 19th century its population was mostly Kurdish, with a significant Jewish minority and smaller numbers of Armenian and Assyrian Christians, mostly Chaldean Catholic. Until 1979 the city had a small Aramaic-speaking Jewish community of about 4,000 people, and its Assyrian community spoke a dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> called Senaya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanandaj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soleyman Khan Ardalan founded <b>Sanandaj</b> (Sinneh) in the 1630s as his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/ardalan">Ardalan</a></li></ul></section>
